# ALTERNATIVE to Cloudflare Tunnel (document only -- nothing here is applied):
# serve OrvaHR through the existing kgf-caddy, which already owns :80/:443.
#
# 1. Put kgf-caddy on OrvaHR's edge network (in the kgf project's compose
#    file, owned by that project):
#        services:
#          caddy:
#            networks: [default, orvahr-edge]
#        networks:
#          orvahr-edge:
#            external: true
#            name: orvahr-saas-edge
#    (one-off equivalent: docker network connect orvahr-saas-edge kgf-caddy-1)
#
# 2. Wildcard certificates need the ACME DNS-01 challenge, which the stock
#    caddy:2-alpine image cannot do. Either build Caddy with a DNS module
#    (xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare) and use the
#    `tls { dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN} }` block below, or use on-demand
#    TLS with an `ask` endpoint that returns 200 only for existing tenants
#    (a new Django view -- not built yet).
#
# 3. Router: forward TCP 80/443 to the Mac mini, and keep the public IP (or
#    DDNS) in DNS. The Mac's IP becomes visible on the internet.
#
# 4. Real client IP: Caddy appends the peer to X-Forwarded-For, so in
#    deploy/saas/nginx/templates/orvahr.conf.template change
#        real_ip_header CF-Connecting-IP;   ->   real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For;
#    (set_real_ip_from stays the edge subnet; real_ip_recursive on). If
#    Cloudflare also proxies in front of Caddy (orange cloud), keep
#    CF-Connecting-IP and add Cloudflare's IP ranges to Caddy's
#    trusted_proxies instead.

orvahr.example, *.orvahr.example {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}
	}
	encode zstd gzip
	request_body {
		max_size 50MB
	}
	reverse_proxy orvahr-saas-nginx-1:80 {
		# Caddy preserves Host by default; keep it (django-tenants needs it).
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
}
